+/*
+ * A34 (0939): Wi-Fi, milestone 1 (docs/wifi-plan.md) -- the MT6877's CONNSYS powered, the
+ * MT6635 A-die found, the Wi-Fi subsystem started and its firmware loaded, all said in the
+ * kernel log. Nothing here runs, and nothing is mapped, unless asked: boot-arg a34wifi=N, or
+ * sysctl kern.a34wifi.want=N (root; userland/a34wifi). Each step needs the ones before it:
+ *
+ *   1  read only: SPM (CONN_PWR_CON, PWR_STATUS), the bus protection, TOPRGU's software
+ *      resets, SRCLKEN_RC's mode, the MT6359P's VCN13/VCN18/VCN33_1/VCN33_2; conn_infra's
+ *      IP version only if the domain is already on and unprotected (then after a wake-up
+ *      request, the one write step 1 may make: conn_infra asleep is not to be read)
+ *   2  conn_infra on, as conninfra.ko's consys_hw_pwr_on() part 1 and 2 (first power-on):
+ *      VCN18 and VCN13 (RC mode or legacy, as the PMIC is set up), the D-die/A-die pins,
+ *      the CONN MTCMOS (clk-mt6877-pg.c: PWR_ON, PWR_ON_2ND, both acks, CLK_DIS, ISO, RST_B),
+ *      TOPRGU's CONNSYS reset released, the bus protection released (CLR registers, step 2
+ *      then step 1), 5 ms for the XO, then IP version 0x02060002 polled
+ *   3  part 3: EMI remap (CONNSYS's memory, see below), d_die_cfg, spi_master_cfg,
+ *      a_die_cfg (MT6635 chip id over the RF SPI, efuse, XO/LDO settings), afe_wbg_cal,
+ *      subsys_pll_initial, the radio status in sysram, low_power_setting (ends with
+ *      conn_infra under hardware sleep control), then Wi-Fi's VCN33_1/VCN33_2
+ *   4  and 5: see the Wi-Fi part below
+ *
+ * Every wait has a limit and says what it read when it gives up; a failed step stops there,
+ * and nothing is undone (the domain stays as the log says). Registers of the 0x18xxxxxx
+ * space are only touched while SPM says the domain is on and its bus protection is off:
+ * reading CONNSYS while it is off hangs the bus.
+ *
+ * The sequences are MediaTek's, read in Samsung's A34 sources (One UI 7, A346BXXSFEZC7,
+ * github.com/Fede2782/android_kernel_samsung_a34x, GPL; read, not copied, the register
+ * values are data): vendor/mediatek/kernel_modules/connectivity/conninfra/conn_drv/connv2/
+ * platform/consys_hw.c (the order), mt6877/mt6877_pos.c (each step, with the addresses in
+ * its comments), mt6877_pmic.c (the rails), mt6877/include/CODA (fields);
+ * kernel_device_modules-6.6/drivers/clk/mediatek/clk-mt6877-pg.c (the MTCMOS) and
+ * drivers/regulator/mt6359p-regulator.c with include/linux/mfd/mt6359p/registers.h (the LDOs).
+ * The phone's device tree (consys@18000000, wifi@18000000) agrees with them. The approach --
+ * a boot-arg per step, each held against what Android does -- is HackiOS's
+ * (bsd/dev/redwood_connsys.c and redwood_mtkwifi.c of github.com/oosexgang/hakios-xnu-kernel,
+ * branch mtk, APSL 2.0), which did the same for the MT6768's CONNSYS; the firmware file reader
+ * follows theirs.
+ *
+ * Memory: XNU manages only RAM above 4 GB (tools/mkadt_a34.py); below it, what the boot loader
+ * reserves is listed in Android's device tree (mblock-*), and none of it lies in 0x50000000..
+ * 0x71ffffff, where Android's kernel places conninfra's 7.5 MB (consys-reserve-memory) and
+ * Wi-Fi's 15 MB (wifi-reserve-memory) at run time. Here they are fixed: CONNSYS's memory
+ * ("EMI") at A34W_EMI_PA, Wi-Fi's DMA memory at A34W_DMA_PA (32-bit DMA: below 4 GB), both
+ * mapped write-combined (normal, uncached) out of kernel_map with ml_io_map_unmappable(), not
+ * the small io_submap; the registers likewise, as device memory.
+ *
+ * Steps 4 and 5 read their firmware from the iOS root, /a34/wifi (tools/wifi-fw.sh takes it from
+ * the phone's own vendor partition; it is never in the repository). See the Wi-Fi part below.
+ */
